1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,155
Authored HTML and Markdown are stored as written, and Orchard Core cleans the

2
00:00:06,155 --> 00:00:08,328
HTML each time it renders it.

3
00:00:08,328 --> 00:00:13,048
This video covers where the HTML Sanitizer applies, how to turn it off

4
00:00:13,048 --> 00:00:16,650
for a part, and how to use and configure it in code.

5
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:24,762
Authors write HTML, or Markdown that becomes HTML.

6
00:00:24,762 --> 00:00:29,591
That markup can hold a script element, an event handler attribute, or a

7
00:00:29,591 --> 00:00:32,037
link whose address runs a script.

8
00:00:32,037 --> 00:00:37,430
Shown as it is, it runs in the visitor's browser, which is cross-site scripting.

9
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:44,136
The HTML Sanitizer cleans the final rendered HTML, each time the content is displayed.

10
00:00:44,136 --> 00:00:47,244
What the author wrote is stored unchanged.

11
00:00:47,244 --> 00:00:53,200
In this example, the script element is removed from the page, and the paragraph stays.

12
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:55,415
The order matters.

13
00:00:55,415 --> 00:01:00,541
A Markdown body is converted to HTML first, then shortcodes are processed, and

14
00:01:00,541 --> 00:01:02,372
the sanitizer runs last.

15
00:01:02,372 --> 00:01:05,850
So markup that a shortcode produces is cleaned too.

16
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:15,512
By default, it applies to the HtmlBodyPart, the HtmlField, the HtmlMenuItemPart, the MarkdownBodyPart, and the MarkdownField.

17
00:01:15,512 --> 00:01:20,819
For the body parts and fields, one setting covers both the rendered page

18
00:01:20,819 --> 00:01:23,540
and the HTML that GraphQL returns.

19
00:01:24,290 --> 00:01:27,043
The sanitizer also checks addresses.

20
00:01:27,043 --> 00:01:32,746
When you save a LinkField, a LinkMenuItemPart, or an HtmlMenuItemPart, the URL is

21
00:01:32,746 --> 00:01:38,645
placed in a link and sanitized, and rejected as invalid if the sanitizer changes it.

22
00:01:38,645 --> 00:01:42,840
Link and HTML menu items are checked again when displayed.

23
00:01:47,022 --> 00:01:52,785
The Content Types page, under Design, Content Definition, lists the content types.

24
00:01:52,785 --> 00:01:54,750
Select Edit for Article.

25
00:01:54,750 --> 00:01:59,072
Its Parts list includes HtmlBody, so select Edit next to it.

26
00:01:59,544 --> 00:02:02,862
Sanitize Html is checked, which is the default.

27
00:02:02,862 --> 00:02:07,808
Its hint says the HTML is sanitized, for example by removing script tags.

28
00:02:07,808 --> 00:02:12,566
Clearing the box turns sanitization off for this part of this type, so

29
00:02:12,566 --> 00:02:15,884
leave it checked unless you trust every author.

30
00:02:16,345 --> 00:02:20,292
The Blog Post type has the same setting on its MarkdownBody part.

31
00:02:20,292 --> 00:02:24,795
There, the hint says it is the HTML output from Markdown that is sanitized.

32
00:02:28,995 --> 00:02:31,881
The sanitizer is available to your own code too.

33
00:02:31,881 --> 00:02:35,034
Call it for any HTML you write to a page that did not

34
00:02:35,034 --> 00:02:37,814
pass through a part or field with the setting.

35
00:02:37,814 --> 00:02:39,845
There are three ways to call it.

36
00:02:40,595 --> 00:02:47,416
In a Razor view, Orchard.SanitizeHtml takes a string of HTML and returns it sanitized.

37
00:02:47,416 --> 00:02:54,015
It returns IHtmlContent, so Razor writes it as markup instead of encoding it again.

38
00:02:54,765 --> 00:03:00,234
In a Liquid template, the sanitize_html filter does the same, and the raw

39
00:03:00,234 --> 00:03:03,072
filter writes the result as markup.

40
00:03:03,072 --> 00:03:07,365
The markdownify filter always sanitizes what it returns.

41
00:03:08,115 --> 00:03:14,222
In your own classes, take an IHtmlSanitizerService in the constructor and call Sanitize.

42
00:03:14,222 --> 00:03:17,665
The Html and Markdown modules call it this way.

43
00:03:21,865 --> 00:03:24,691
AddOrchardCms registers the sanitizer.

44
00:03:24,691 --> 00:03:29,445
On top of the defaults of its library, Orchard Core allows the class

45
00:03:29,445 --> 00:03:35,805
attribute, allows the mailto and tel schemes, and removes the form tag from the allowed tags.

46
00:03:36,555 --> 00:03:41,880
To change the rules, call ConfigureHtmlSanitizer on the tenant services, with an action

47
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:43,827
that receives the sanitizer.

48
00:03:43,827 --> 00:03:45,946
This one allows the ftp scheme.

49
00:03:45,946 --> 00:03:49,611
You can call it more than once, and the actions run in the

50
00:03:49,611 --> 00:03:51,215
order they were added.

51
00:03:51,965 --> 00:03:56,765
The link checks use the same sanitizer, so they accept the schemes you allow here.

52
00:03:57,515 --> 00:04:02,404
Sanitized results are cached for fragments up to 64K characters, and a tenant

53
00:04:02,404 --> 00:04:05,055
never receives another tenant's result.

54
00:04:05,055 --> 00:04:11,653
So whatever you configure with ConfigureHtmlSanitizer, including the sanitizer event handlers, must return

55
00:04:11,653 --> 00:04:14,245
the same result for the same fragment.

56
00:04:16,245 --> 00:04:18,103
That's the HTML Sanitizer:

57
00:04:18,103 --> 00:04:23,735
authored content is stored as written, cleaned when it is rendered, and configured in code.

58
00:04:23,735 --> 00:04:25,245
Thanks for watching.

